\section{Introduction}
Bathari (Ba\d{t}\d{h}ari, ISO 639-3 bhm) is a Modern South Arabian language of the Afro-Asiatic family, spoken on the south-eastern coast of Oman. Sources in the search results describe it as nearly extinct, with very few speakers (Wikipedia gives 16 native speakers for 2016), and as closely related to Mehri; some members of the Bathari tribe speak only Mehri. No school materials in or for Bathari were found.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes for Bathari were found in the search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia overview of the Bathari language: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ba%E1%B9%AD%E1%B8%A5ari_language}
\item DICE (University of Missouri) Afro-Asiatic questionnaire on Bathari: \url{https://dice.missouri.edu/assets/docs/afro-asiatic/Bathari.pdf}
\item Endangered Languages Project entry for Bathari: \url{https://www.endangeredlanguages.com/lang/bhm}
\end{itemize}
A search summary mentions a 2025 descriptive grammar by Miranda Morris and Fabio Gasparini, but no page for it was seen, so no URL is given.

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
No audio, video or corpus resources were found in the search results.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
With so few speakers and no known teaching materials, any curriculum would need to begin with documentation work with the remaining speakers. Related Mehri resources may be relevant but were not searched here.
\end{document}
